#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078200 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/id #uuid "a276ef48-ee77-5113-bbb1-817ce6c31e75" :question/math-academy-id "e-3593" :question/problem "On which intervals is the function $y= f(x)$ below continuous?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/0a43ec719ede0b6c3b31fb585b52e514.png)" :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-2,1)$ and $(1,2)$. There is a discontinuity at $x=1$, but this is already excluded.\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-1$, so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous in the intervals $(-2,-1)$, $(-1,1)$, and $(1,2)$. It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078201 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/id #uuid "7c67eb12-1841-5855-b6b6-4c2587c9dbd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3594" :question/problem "On which intervals is the function $y= f(x)$ below continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/f51fc708b4c8a9aaaec9baa0c6c07a4d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty, -2)$, $(-2,1)$, and $(1,7)$.\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=1$ and left-continuous at $x=7$. Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[1,7]$.\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty,-2)$, $(-2,1)$, and $[1,7]$. It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078248 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/id #uuid "52452253-0dbc-5dbe-92c4-05f9e050a100" :question/math-academy-id "e-6161" :question/problem "Determine the intervals in which the function $y= f(x)$ below is continuous. You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/ded1e07424bbf156ab03101610734718.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty,-1)$, $(-1,1)$ and $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-1$. Hence, it is continuous on the interval $[MATH: [-1,1).]$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty,-1)$, $[MATH: [-1,1)]$ and $(1,\\infty)$. It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078249 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a950dcd4-ab41-526c-9ae3-c8fe471fcff4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6162" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/80b16a16a84dcd00a43c21b0c1cf96ac.png)\n\n1. $x\\in (-\\infty, -3)$\n2. $x\\in (-2,3)$\n3. $[MATH: x\\in [3,\\infty)]$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty,-3)$. It is continuous on $[MATH: (-\\infty, -2]]$ and this contains the interval $(-\\infty,-3)$.\n- The function is continuous on the open interval $(-2,3)$.\n- The function is continuous on $[MATH: [3, \\infty).]$ It is continuous on $(3,\\infty)$ and also right continuous at $x=3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "918d5704-c936-55d5-8203-eac0ffb33b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076660} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0d0ca5-e6b9-5a9b-9739-4d0d30653d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076661} {:answer/id #uuid "efa4479b-f8c0-5b64-9dd7-acfb425f9f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076662} {:answer/id #uuid "004d454c-d47a-5a5b-a963-7444ffec7a88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076663} {:answer/id #uuid "561a3430-7984-5ce3-8fad-90a652ccf2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "561a3430-7984-5ce3-8fad-90a652ccf2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48293-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076664} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3688b07-cbb6-5b89-9be0-3e7661ec0fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076659}] :question/id #uuid "b9688522-541f-5cbf-8ad0-7cde48d13